course: dictionary
lesson: 5349
title: Kilo: a word with a thousand inside
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A kilo is short for kilogram, a unit of weight. A kilogram is a thousand grams, so a kilo is a lot of weight for one bag of apples, not a little. Shops say «a kilo of apples» or «price per kilo» when they mean weight.
explain: The little word kilo- at the start of a longer word is a prefix, and it means a thousand. A kilometre is a thousand metres, and a kilowatt is a thousand watts. When you see kilo- at the start of a word, think thousand, then check the rest of the word.
explain: Context helps again. «One big parrotfish can make hundreds of kilos of sand a year.» Hundreds of kilos is a huge amount, and sand is measured by weight here, so kilos must mean units of weight. People also use kilo informally for kilometre, but in maths keep kilo for weight and remember kilo- for a thousand.
fact: A kilo is short for kilogram.
fact: One kilogram is a thousand grams.
fact: The prefix kilo- means a thousand, as in kilometre and kilowatt.
fact: «The giant clam can weigh more than two hundred kilos.» Here kilos measure weight.
fact: In French, kilo is masculine, so you say «un kilo de pommes» (a kilo of apples).
exercise: In «a kilo of apples», what does kilo measure? | *Weight, a thousand grams | Money, like a price | Time, a thousand seconds | Length, a thousand metres
exercise: The prefix kilo- means what? | Ten | A hundred | Half | *A thousand
exercise: «One big parrotfish can make hundreds of kilos of sand a year.» What do the kilos measure here? | Length | Speed | *Weight | Time
test: A kilometre is a thousand what? | Seconds | *Metres | Grams | Watts
test: How many grams make one kilo? | 100 | 10 | *1000 | 10000
exam: Kilo is masculine in French, so which phrase is correct for "a kilo of apples"? | une kilo de pomme | *un kilo de pommes | un kilos de pommes | une kilo de pommes
